---
/**
 * /adult-drivers-ed/texas/first-texas-license-18-to-24 — the required segment (TOFU into the course).
 *
 * Rebuilt 2026-10-13 on the hook → answer → loop → funnel recipe (ADE-PAGE-RECIPE.md). Query family: "texas
 * drivers license 18-24", "requirements to get a texas drivers license at 18", "texas driver's license
 * requirements 18 year old", "first texas license at 18". The answer is the §521.1601 rule in one breath; the
 * loop it opens is the order of the rest (course, documents, appointment, ITAD, tests), because the order is
 * what keeps the 90-day ITAD certificate valid. The band closes on "do it before you book DPS".
 *
 * Facts: src/data/ade-eligibility.ts, src/data/ade-texas.json. §84.40(j): never promise a license; exam wording is
 * CLAIM only.
 */
import AdeGuide from '../../../components/ade/AdeGuide.astro';
import ade from '../../../data/ade-texas.json';
import { law, itd, fees, residency, process as dpsProcess, CLAIM, WAIVER_LAPSE } from '../../../data/ade-eligibility';

const base = '/adult-drivers-ed/texas';

// The one-breath answer. Same sentence in the meta description and the lede.
const answer = `At 18 to 24, Texas requires a six-hour drivers ed course before DPS can issue your first license (Transp. Code §521.1601), unless you hold a valid license from another U.S. state.`;

const steps = [
  { t: `Finish the six-hour course.`, b: `You get the ${ade.course.code} certificate, which is what you bring to DPS. Passing the course ${CLAIM}.` },
  { t: 'Gather your documents.', b: `Identity, lawful presence and ${residency.documentsRequired} Texas residency documents. ${residency.thirtyDayRule}` },
  { t: 'Book the DPS appointment.', b: 'DPS serves driver license applicants by appointment, and the road-test queue depends on the office you pick.' },
  { t: `Watch the ${itd.adultLengthHours}-hour ITAD video.`, b: `${itd.adultName} must be done before the driving skills test, and the certificate is good for ${itd.certValidDays} days. Watch it once you have a test date.` },
  { t: 'Take the tests at DPS.', b: `Pay the $${fees.newLicense18to84} fee and take the vision test and the driving skills test. The card follows in ${dpsProcess.cardArrivesWeeks} weeks.` },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do 18 to 24 year olds need drivers ed in Texas?', a: `Yes, for a first driver license. Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601 bars DPS from issuing a license to anyone under ${law.ageCeiling} without a driver education certificate, and DPS applies that as a six-hour adult driver education course for applicants ${law.requiredBand}. The exception is if you already hold a valid license issued by another U.S. state.` },
  { q: 'What are the requirements to get a Texas driver license at 18?', a: `For a first license at ${law.requiredBand}: the six-hour adult driver education course, the ${itd.adultName} video within ${itd.certValidDays} days before your driving test, identity, lawful presence and Texas residency documents, the $${fees.newLicense18to84} DPS fee, and the vision and driving skills tests at DPS.` },
  { q: 'How long is the adult drivers ed course in Texas?', a: 'Six hours, which Texas defines as 360 minutes with a minimum of 330 minutes of instruction. It is online and self-paced, so you can do it across several sittings rather than in one block.' },
  { q: 'Does the course replace the DPS written test?', a: `It ${CLAIM}. Under §521.167 you are not required to take those parts of the examination once a licensed driver education instructor confirms you passed them. The vision test and the driving skills test still happen at DPS. ${WAIVER_LAPSE}` },
  { q: 'What else do I need besides the course?', a: `The ${itd.adultName} video before your driving skills test, your identity, lawful presence and Texas residency documents, and the $${fees.newLicense18to84} DPS fee. The course is one requirement among several, not the whole process.` },
  { q: 'What happens when I turn 25 partway through?', a: 'If you apply at 25 or older, there is no driver education requirement, because the requirement is tested when DPS issues the license. If you are close to 25 and not in a hurry, you can wait. If you need to drive sooner, the six hours is the faster path.' },
];

const next = [
  { href: `${base}/how-to-get-a-texas-license-as-an-adult`, title: 'The whole process, step by step', blurb: 'Each step with the requirement behind it, so nothing surprises you at the counter.' },
  { href: `${base}/texas-driver-license-documents`, title: 'What to bring to the DPS appointment', blurb: 'The documents DPS asks for, and the residency rule that catches most people.' },
  { href: `${base}/impact-texas-adult-drivers`, title: 'The 90-day ITAD certificate trap', blurb: 'Why the free DPS video is the last thing you do before the road test.' },
  { href: `${base}/cost`, title: 'What the course costs', blurb: 'One price with the exam and the certificate in it, and the DPS fee on top.' },
];
---

<AdeGuide
  title="Texas Driver License at 18 to 24: Six-Hour Drivers Ed Required First | Road Ready Safety"
  description={answer}
  url={`${base}/first-texas-license-18-to-24`}
  eyebrow="Eligibility · Required at 18 to 24"
  h1="Getting your first Texas license at 18 to 24? The law puts a six-hour course first"
  lede={`${answer} After the course come four more steps, and their order decides whether your ITAD certificate is still valid on the day of your driving test. The order is below.`}
  faqs={faqs}
  dateModified="2026-10-13"
  updated="October 13, 2026"
  band={{ eyebrow: 'Required at 18 to 24' }}
  next={next}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601, Driver Education Required; Exception', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.167, Waiver of Certain Education and Examination Requirements', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Apply for a Texas Driver License', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/apply-texas-driver-license' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Impact Texas Drivers (ITD) Program', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/impact-texas-drivers-itd-program' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Do you need drivers ed at 18 to get a Texas license?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Yes, if it is your first license and you do not hold a valid license from another U.S. state.
      §521.1601 bars DPS from issuing a license to anyone younger than {law.ageCeiling} without a
      driver education certificate.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Texas has required driver education for teenagers for a long time. In 2010 it extended the idea
      upward, and the intent was that people who missed driver education in high school should not
      simply age out of it. A license from another country, or an out-of-state license that expired
      long ago, changes the answer in ways people do not expect;{' '}<a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/do-i-need-adult-drivers-ed`}>the rule by age and license</a>{' '}sorts every case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">In what order do you do everything?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Course first, then documents, then the DPS appointment, then the ITAD video, then the tests. The
      video goes late because its certificate lasts {itd.certValidDays} days and the road-test queue at
      the slowest offices can be longer than that (<a class="underline hover:no-underline" href={`${base}/dps-wait-times`}>current waits by office</a>).
    </p>
    <ol class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-decimal marker:text-text-subtle">
      {steps.map((s) => (
        <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><strong>{s.t}</strong>{' '}{s.b}</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What is in the six hours?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Texas defines the course as 360 minutes, of which at least 330 must be instruction and the
      balance may be breaks. It is self-paced, so you are not sitting in one six-hour block unless you
      want to. The content is set by the state, not the provider: licensing and registration,
      right-of-way, signs and signals, controlling traffic flow, alcohol and other drugs, sharing the
      road, managing risk, and a final examination on highway signs and traffic laws.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      The adult version is shorter than the teen course (six hours rather than 32), and there is no
      behind-the-wheel component and no parent involvement. It is a classroom requirement delivered
      online.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">What does finishing the course change at DPS?</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      It {CLAIM}. Under §521.167, finishing an approved course means you are not required to take the
      highway sign and traffic law parts of the DPS examination, once a licensed driver education
      instructor confirms you passed them.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      You still take the vision test. You still take the driving skills test, and that is the part
      with the queue. And no course can promise you a license.
    </p>
  </section>
</AdeGuide>
